;;; A string written in a hypothesis rule's `:assert` proposes nothing —
;;; regression `str-in-hrule` (`corpus/regressions.toml`), the
;;; case's mechanism in a hypothesis rule.
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two `holds` facts, over the names `1` and `a`, and one hypothesis rule that
;;; proposes tagging each with the string `"lbl"`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A string is natural-language text, never a value (Q-M1e2.23), written
;;; only after `:why`, `:source` or `:goal-text`: the program is refused at
;;; the parse, at its first string, on every command.
;;;
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and what it answers ──────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `ff023413`, in a release build and a debug one, R was `{∅}`: the rule
;;; compiler did not lower a string, so the hypothesis rule's template held an opaque
;;; slot, which builds no fact, and the hypothesis layer dropped a template that
;;; builds none in silence. H was empty: `ein solve FILE -H`
;;; printed *no candidate: R is {∅} or empty, decided by root alone*. Today
;;; the program is refused at the parse: *unexpected `"lbl"`: a string*.
;;;

(relation holds Node) (relation tag Node Node)
(holds 1) (holds a)
(rule h () :match (holds ?x) :assert (open (tag ?x "lbl")))
